      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Downloading or distributing ROM copies of those games without Nintendo's permission infringes that copyright.",
      "evidence": "U.S. Copyright Act §106 gives the copyright holder exclusive rights to reproduce and distribute the work; unauthorized copying is infringement (e.g., Sega v. Accolade, 2001).",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The DMCA prohibits circumvention of the copy‑protection mechanisms used in Nintendo cartridges, even for personal backup.",
      "evidence": "17 U.S.C. §1201 makes it illegal to bypass technological protection measures; cases such as Universal City Studios v. Reimer (2004) upheld this prohibition for video‑game media.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Using a ROM you do not lawfully own (i.e., without having purchased the original cartridge and made a legal dump) is illegal.",
      "evidence": "Copyright law permits making a copy only with the right holder’s permission or a statutory exception; the lack of ownership plus the anti‑circumvention rule makes such use unlawful.",
